<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Musta'li</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1074">1074</a>–1101</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Musta'li (1074–1101) was the ninth Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, ruling from 1094 to 1101. A younger son of al-Mustansir, he was placed on the throne by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a> in preference to his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizar</a>, splitting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> movement into Musta'li and Nizari branches. During his reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and the crusaders took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in 1099.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mustali.jpg" alt="Al-Musta'li" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, 11th/12th-century mint master · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dinar_of_al-Musta%27li_billah,_AH_493.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abū al-Qāsim Aḥmad ibn al-Mustanṣir was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, most probably on 15 or 16 September 1074, to the eighth Fatimid caliph, al-Mustansir Billah. He was most likely the youngest of his father's sons. An older brother born in 1060 had the same name, and some later sources confuse the two. At the time the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> had lost most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, and fighting between Turkish and black African troops had brought famine and anarchy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In January 1074 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-jamali">Badr al-Jamali</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and restored order, at the price of taking over the government, army and religious and judicial administration from the caliph.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Al-Mustansir died on 29 December 1094. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizar</a>, the eldest son, was widely expected to succeed, though no formal designation of him is recorded. Shortly before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s death Ahmad had married Sitt al-Mulk, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-jamali">Badr al-Jamali</a>, and Badr's son, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal-shahanshah">al-Afdal Shahanshah</a>, put his brother-in-law Ahmad on the throne as al-Mustaʿlī biʾllāh. Nizar fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, proclaimed himself caliph and held out against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a> until the city fell. He was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and immured, and he died there. Modern historians such as Farhad Daftary regard the accession as a de facto coup by al-Afdal, who wanted a compliant figurehead.</p><p>The dispute split the Isma'ili movement for good. The Fatimid establishment and the Isma'ili communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> accepted al-Musta'li, but most communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, under Hassan-i Sabbah, recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizar</a> and formed the Nizari branch. Al-Musta'li remained subordinate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a> throughout his reign. The Egyptian historian Ibn Muyassar wrote that al-Afdal directed state affairs like a sultan, not a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. Egypt stayed prosperous under al-Afdal's administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Abroad, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> faced the Sunni Seljuks and the Abbasid caliph al-Mustazhir. They recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> in 1097 and, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from its Artuqid rulers in July or August 1098. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> captured Jerusalem on 15 July 1099, and a Fatimid army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a> was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ascalon">Battle of Ascalon</a> on 12 August 1099. Many Syrians fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where a famine broke out in 1099 or 1100.</p><p>Al-Musta'li died on 11 or 12 December 1101, amid rumours that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a> had poisoned him. He left three infant sons, and the eldest, al-Mansur, not yet five, was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> as al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a>. The Musta'li branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ilism</a> takes its name from him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Musta'li” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the succession crisis of 1094, Vizier al-Afdal bypassed the designated heir Nizar in favour of al-<b>Musta'li</b>, which permanently split the Ismaili community and destroyed the da'wa's universal authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1091" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Al-Musta'li"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5091 4996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5091 4996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1091">What was here in 1091? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
